{{also|telescòpic}}
==English==
[[File:Negretti_zambra_telescope_2.jpg|thumb|200px|A [[retractable]] telescope ([[spyglass]]). Sense 4 arose by reference to such devices.]]

===Etymology===
From {{confix|en|tele|scopic}}, after {{m|en|telescope}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/tɛlɪˈskɒpɪk/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-telescopic.wav|Audio (UK)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɒpɪk|s=4}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Pertaining to, or carried out by means of, a [[telescope]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2015|author=David Wootton|title=The Invention of Science|page=197|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2016
|passage=Within a year or two of Galileo's '''telescopic''' discoveries no one disputed that the moon had mountains, Jupiter had moons, Venus had phases and the sun had spots […].}}
# {{lb|en|chiefly|astronomy}} Seen by means of a telescope; only visible through a telescope. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#: '''''telescopic''' stars''
# [[capable|Capable]] of [[seeing]] [[distant]] [[object]]s; far-seeing. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
# Able to be [[extended]] or [[retracted]] by the use of [[part]]s that [[slide]] over one another. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#: {{uxi|en|telescopic baton}}
# Referring to parts being [[extended]] or [[retracted]] along coinciding axes (with or without direct contact between the parts). {{defdate|from 20th c.}}

